Across the declared multiplier range, the largest mean normalized vertical
error is \OURobustnessWorstMean\% of $H_s$ in the
\OURobustnessWorstParameter{} direction at
$\times\OURobustnessWorstScale$, differing from nominal by
\OURobustnessWorstDifference{} percentage points
($[\OURobustnessWorstDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessWorstDifferenceHigh]$).
The sweeps also show competing objectives: changing $r_S$ can improve the
vertical score while changing 3D displacement, and changing $\sigma_{aw}$ can
alter pitch even when vertical displacement changes little.  Accordingly, no sweep point is adopted as a replacement tuning; the sweep is interpreted as
local sensitivity rather than an offline search for a new operating point.

\paragraph{Why the direct $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ effects are weak}
With their companion parameters frozen, the $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ sweeps
change mean vertical error by only \OURobustnessTauSpan{} and
\OURobustnessSigmaSpan{} percentage points over the full multiplier range,
whereas $r_S$ changes it by \OURobustnessRSSpan{} points and the coupled sweeps
show the sensitivity of the deployed tuning law.  This does not indicate an
unused OU parameter.  The accelerometer directly observes the latent
acceleration at \SI{200}{Hz} with measurement noise small relative to its
stationary variance, so the latent-acceleration gain is already close to
saturation and moderate prior rescaling has little effect on integrated
vertical displacement.  The unobserved low-frequency drift channel remains
sensitive to $r_S$.  Attitude is different because the latent-acceleration
covariance also enters the accelerometer innovation covariance, explaining the
measurable pitch sensitivity to $\sigma_{aw}$.

At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the mean absolute vertical RMS error is
\SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, corresponding to
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$; the increase in normalized error exposes
the approximately fixed absolute sensor-error floor.  Compressing the
transition from \SI{360}{s} to \SI{30}{s} changes the Adaptive mean from
\OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of the final $H_s$.  This rapid-case difference
is a degradation result rather than a pure adaptation-rate measurement because
compressing the crossfade also changes the composition of the fixed
\SI{900}{s} scoring window.  Isolating adaptation bandwidth would require a
protocol that holds the scored sea-state composition fixed while varying only
the transition rate.
